{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"magnesian-subjective-ocelot","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEvaluating a Pass-1 change on Sonnet when production runs Opus (or vice versa) confounds the measurement: prior work showed Sonnet degrades past ~3 criteria per turn while Opus is flat, so a delta could be the model, not the change. Two further hazards: the production model classes (`rebar.toml` `[llm.model_classes]`: frontier = `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, standard = `bedrock:…claude-sonnet-4-6`) carry an Anthropic-direct fallback, and `_intersect_capabilities` (`src/rebar/llm/runner_support.py`) collapses a mixed chain's `prompt_cache_style` to `none` — which is why cache reads dropped to zero on 2026-08-12 and review cost roughly doubled. An eval run that silently fell back to Anthropic would both break the \"Bedrock only\" requirement and lose caching.\n\n## Approach\n\nBuild `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/parity.py` and wire it into every tier:\n\n- **Pinned targets**: the harness resolves the model for each pass from the production class but requires the resolved id to be a Bedrock id and drops any fallback candidate (homogeneous single-provider chain). It refuses to start when the resolved id differs from the corpus baseline's `provider_provenance.ran_model` for that pass, unless `--allow-model-change` is passed, in which case the run is labelled `model-change` and can never be diffed against a baseline with a different id.\n- **Result stamping**: every result row carries `{pass: model_id}`; `report.py` refuses to diff two result sets whose per-pass ids differ.\n- **Caching check**: after each run, assert from the run's usage-log rows that `cache_read_tokens > 0` on at least one call after the first; the report states cached vs uncached and the ledger flags uncached runs (they cost ~2× and should be investigated before the next run).\n- No production config is changed by this ticket; the homogeneous chain is composed in-process for eval runs only.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/parity.py` (new); hooks in `tier1.py`, `tier2.py`, `report.py`\n- `tests/unit/evals/test_plan_replay_parity.py` (new)\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit: a config whose frontier class resolves to an Anthropic id is refused; a mixed chain is reduced to its Bedrock candidate; diffing results with different Pass-2 model ids raises; a usage-log fixture with zero cache reads marks the run uncached. Live check happens as part of the Tier-1 reproduction run.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every Tier-1/Tier-2 result and ledger row carries Bedrock model ids equal to the production class for that pass; checked by the ledger after commissioning.\n- [ ] A diff between result sets with differing per-pass model ids is refused; checked by a unit test.\n- [ ] The Tier-1 reproduction run shows `cache_read_tokens > 0`; checked from its usage-log rows and stated in its report.\n- [ ] Unit tests pass in `make test`.","id":"efd4-847d-f7ad-47bc","parent_id":"759e-7dc1-675c-4877","priority":1,"tags":["plan-review","evals","bedrock","model-parity"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Model-parity guard: pin each eval pass to its production model on a homogeneous Bedrock chain; confirm prompt caching"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788029193534685748,"uuid":"b467d435-dc96-4655-84cb-7ddd44662b55"}